# Data loading from DB

## Purpose
  Load data from DB for block list and Robot & Operation profile selection.
  Update BlockListRobot.json when block data changes in the DB.
  Robot Type and Operation Profile dropdowns are always loaded live from DB at app startup.
  This sync is triggered by the button 'Update Blocks' (also runs automatically at startup).

## Conditions
  DB connection parameters are set in dbinfo.json.
  Create DB library for CRUD with sql query. It should be a different file.
  Use DB library to load data for block list from the table.
  Update only the data scopes specified below, 'Block list' part.
  Do not update unrelated categories/items.
  Before writing DB values, remove only the existing values that belong to the same DB-managed scope.
  Keep all non-targeted existing values as-is.
  If target table has no rows or no applicable data, keep current values for that target scope.

## display_name column
  Each DB table may have an optional `display_name` column.
  - If the column exists: use it as the human-readable label for UI dropdowns.
  - If the column is absent or the value is empty: fall back to `name` as the display label.
  - The `value` field in generated options always stores the raw DB `name` (used for BT generation).
  - Blockly dropdowns are built as [[display_name, value], ...] pairs so the UI shows
    display_name while Export/BT-generation uses value.

## robot_type / operation_profile columns (block-level filtering)
  Block option rows in DB may have optional `robot_type` and `operation_profile` columns.
  - If present and non-empty: the option is only shown when the active profile matches.
  - If absent or empty: the option is a "common" option shown regardless of profile.
  - These columns are written into the block option metadata in BlockListRobot.json
    and propagated into the generated JS (BLOCK_PROFILE, OPTION_PROFILE_META).

## Robot & Operation profile

  ### UI panel: "Robot & Operation"
    - First panel on the main page (before Scenario and Blocks panels).
    - Two dropdowns:
        Robot Type  — populated from RobotType table
        Operation   — populated from OperationProfile table
    - "Apply" button sets the active profile and immediately re-filters the Blockly toolbox.
    - Changing the Robot Type dropdown immediately applies the profile without clicking Apply.
    - Active profile is persisted in localStorage (key: bric_profile) and restored on reload.
    - When opening a scenario saved with a different profile, a toast offers to apply it.

  ### API endpoint: GET /api/profile/options
    - Queries DB live on every call — no file cache.
    - Returns: { ok, robot_types: [{value, display_name}], operation_profiles: [{value, display_name}] }
    - If DB is unreachable or tables are absent, returns empty lists (no error).

  ### RobotType table
    Columns:
      name           — internal value used in profile matching
      display_name   — human-readable label (optional; fallback: name)

  ### OperationProfile table
    Columns:
      name           — internal value
      display_name   — human-readable label (optional; fallback: name)

  ### RobotList table
    Not used in the UI. Only Robot Type and Operation are presented to the user.

  ### Scenario profile storage
    Saved scenarios wrap blocks in: { "profile": { "robot_type": "...", "operation_profile": "..." }, "blocks": {...} }
    Old format (blocks only) is detected and loaded without profile (backward-compatible).
    GET /api/scenarios/<name>/blockly returns the saved profile alongside workspace data.

## Category filtering by Robot Type

  ### Overview
    The Blockly toolbox categories are filtered based on the selected Robot Type.
    Only categories allowed for the active robot type are shown in the toolbox.
    If no robot type is selected (All), all categories are shown.
    If the DB returns no categories for a robot type, all categories are shown (no filter).

  ### Default categories (always shown regardless of DB filter)
    The following categories are structural and always visible:
      BT Logic, BT Function, Scenario, Functions

  ### Empty category rule
    After block-level and category-level filtering, any category with no remaining
    blocks is hidden from the toolbox.

  ### API endpoint: GET /api/blocks/categories
    - Accepts optional query param: ?robot_type=<value>
    - Returns: { ok, categories: ["motion", "navigation", ...] }
    - Empty result (no matching rows) → no filter applied, all categories shown.
    - If DB is unreachable, returns empty list (all categories shown as fallback).

  ### BehaviorCategory table
    Columns:
      name             — category name (matches category field in BehaviorCapability, lowercase)
      display_name     — human-readable label
      robot_type_list  — JSON array of robot type values, e.g. ["q9", "cloid"]
                         Use "common" to match all robot types: ["common"]
                         A category is shown if robot_type_list contains the active
                         robot type OR contains "common".

  ### Frontend behavior (static/app.js)
    - allowedCategories state: null = no filter; array = allowed category names for active robot type.
    - fetchCategoryFilter(robotType) calls /api/blocks/categories and updates allowedCategories.
    - applyActiveProfile() is async: fetches category filter first, then refreshes toolbox.
    - Robot Type dropdown change event immediately triggers applyActiveProfile().
    - Category name matching is case-insensitive.

## Block list

  ### Normal Blocks
    Table: BehaviorCapability
    Columns:
      category:    category name (matches BehaviorCategory.name, lowercase)
      name:        action name (matches blockly function name, e.g. 'motion/start_motion')
      parameters:  JSON array of parameter definitions
      description: description text (may contain \r\n; normalized to \n on read)
      robot_type:  robot_type

  ### Description normalization
    DB description values may contain Windows-style line endings (\r\n).
    These are normalized to \n in two places:
    - _fetch_behavior_capability_rows() in db_sync.py: before writing to BlockListRobot.json
    - clean_text() in generator_engine.py: before embedding in generated JS
    This ensures the help popup (which uses `white-space: pre-wrap`) displays correctly.

  ### Parameter Extension
    - category: motion
      action: 'motion/start_motion'
        target: parameters[].task_type.options[].value: 'expressive_motion'
          DB info for parameters:
            Table: MotionExpressive
            Columns
              value: name
              display_name: display_name  (fallback: name)
              description: description
              robot_type: robot_type      (default: "", for robot type filtering)
              operation_profile: operation_profile  (default:"", for profile filtering)
        target: parameters[].task_type.options[].value: 'pose_motion'
          DB info for parameters:
            Table: MotionPose
            Columns
              value: name
              display_name: display_name  (fallback: name)
              description: description
              robot_type: robot_type      (default: "", for robot type filtering)
              operation_profile: operation_profile  (default:"", for profile filtering)

